# Detection of Kinematic Sunyaev–Zel'dovich Signals Using the Pairwise Momentum Estimator

## Research Overview

This repository contains the Python code developed for the MSc research project investigating the detection of the kinematic Sunyaev–Zel'dovich (kSZ) signal using the pairwise momentum estimator.

The analysis uses galaxy data from the Dark Energy Spectroscopic Instrument (DESI) D56 catalogue and temperature data from the Atacama Cosmology Telescope (ACT).

## Objectives

The main objectives of the analysis are:

- To measure the pairwise kSZ signal as a function of comoving separation.
- To compare the measured signal with theoretical predictions.
- To estimate the statistical uncertainties using bootstrap and jackknife resampling.
- To constrain the effective optical depth by fitting the theoretical pairwise kSZ model to the measurements.

## Methodology

The analysis implements the pairwise momentum estimator to investigate the relative peculiar velocities of galaxy pairs and their associated kSZ temperature signal.

The analysis includes:

1. Preparation and processing of the DESI D56 galaxy catalogue.
2. Selection of galaxy pairs and calculation of their comoving separations.
3. Measurement of the pairwise kSZ signal using ACT temperature data.
4. Theoretical modelling of the pairwise velocity signal.
5. Covariance estimation using bootstrap and jackknife resampling.
6. Optical-depth estimation through comparison with theoretical predictions.

## Data

The analysis uses the DESI D56 galaxy catalogue and ACT D56 temperature maps.

The original observational datasets are not included in this repository. They must be obtained separately from their respective data sources.

The data file paths in the Python code may need to be adjusted to match the user's local environment.

## Software and Tools

The analysis was developed using Python and scientific computing libraries for numerical calculations, data processing, cosmological modelling and statistical analysis.

## Reproducibility

The Python code is provided to document the analysis procedures and support the reproducibility and independent examination of the research.

Users wishing to reproduce the results must obtain the required observational datasets and configure the corresponding file paths.

